<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Raj Singh I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1629">1629</a>–<a href="/years/1680">1680</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia-dynasty">Sisodia dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">Raj</a> Singh I was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharana">Maharana</a> of Mewar from 1652 to 1680. He opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>'s reimposition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a> in 1679 and supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rathore">Rathores of Marwar</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> war against the Mughal emperor. He gave shelter to the image of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrinathji">Shrinathji</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nathdwara">Nathdwara</a> in 1672 and built the great reservoir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajsamand-lake">Rajsamand Lake</a>, whose embankment carries the Raj Prashasti, one of the longest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> inscriptions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raj-singh-i.jpg" alt="Raj Singh I" width="320" height="476" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raja Ravi Varma · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raja_Ravi_Varma,_Maharana_Raj_Singh_-_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Raj Singh was born on 24 September 1629, the eldest son of Maharana Jagat Singh I of Mewar, and succeeded his father in 1652. During the Mughal war of succession all the Mughal princes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> among them, asked him to send troops in their support. He stayed aloof and ignored Aurangzeb's repeated demands, and instead led expeditions of his own under the pretext of a Tikadaur, a ceremonial raid traditionally made into enemy land.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In May 1658 he fell on Mughal posts, imposing levies on places such as Mandal, Banera, Shahpura, Jahazpur and Phulia and annexing some areas, then plundered Malpura, Tonk, Chaksu, Lalsot and Sambhar before returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a>. In 1659 he attacked Dungarpur, Banswara and Devaliya, former dependencies of Mewar that had come under Mughal suzerainty, and their rulers accepted Mewar's overlordship. He also raided Mughal Malwa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, and his son Prince Bhim Singh captured Idar.</p><p>He opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> more than once, saving the Kishangarh princess Charumati from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> and denouncing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a>. Chatrapati <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a> once taunted Aurangzeb in a letter, challenging him to demand the tax from the Rana of Mewar rather than from unarmed subjects. When Aurangzeb interfered in the succession of Marwar after the death of Jaswant Singh, Raj Singh, a relative of Ajit Singh of Marwar, aided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durgadas-rathore">Durgadas Rathore</a> and fought the emperor in the war that followed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Raj Singh was a patron of art, music and architecture. He gave protection to the priests and the image of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrinathji">Shrinathji</a> brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> and installed it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nathdwara">Nathdwara</a>. In 1676 he built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajsamand-lake">Rajsamand Lake</a>, also called Rajsamudra, at Kankroli, whose water relieved famine-stricken farmland, and he commissioned the text of the Raj Prashasti later inscribed on pillars around it. He died on 22 October 1680 at the village of Oda, poisoned by nobles bribed by the Mughal court. His confidant, the warrior-poet Charan Askaran Dadhivadia, jagirdar of Oda, ate the same food and died with him; their cenotaphs stand at Oda. His son Jai Singh succeeded him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Raj Singh I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Successive rulers built some of the largest reservoirs of pre-modern India: Lake Pichola was enlarged when Udai Singh II founded Udaipur, <b>Raj Singh I</b> created Rajsamand Lake in the 1660s and 1670s, and Jai Singh built Jaisamand Lake in the late seventeenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=24.58,73.68&amp;year=1662" aria-label="Udaipur on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6508 1259 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Raj Singh I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6958 1529h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6958 1529h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=24.58,73.68&amp;year=1662">What was here in 1662? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
